From: freeman@netcom.com (Jay Reynolds Freeman)
Date: Wed, 7 Dec 1994 00:30:23 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-263] BASICS: Environment and markets

[tburns and John Bozeman discuss pollution safeguards in a laissez-faire
 economy... tburns says:]

> As you point out [pollution safguards] is not a problem that the status
> quo has solved.  A more free system will encourage more innovation.

  Not to doubt the last sentence, but this discussion begins to tread
on the interesting issue of what a laissez-faire economy might provide
in ways to deal with resources which are not subdividable, or nearly
so, due to technical limitations, and on the closely related question
of how to address encroachment on and damage to persons and property
by substances whose origin is difficult to trace.  Resources difficult
to subdivide appear to include air (in the atmosphere) and water (in
oceans, perhaps in lakes, and perhaps in aquifers).  Pollutants which
move through them are of concern to many, and their origin is often
hard to establish precisely, even when there are obviously only a small
number of possible sources (eg, smokestacks).
    Persons who believe in dynamic optimism will no doubt conclude
that technology will solve any such difficulties, but unless they also
believe that the solution will appear instantly, there is more to
discuss:  Do would-be libertarians or anarchocapitalists have any 
mechanism consistent with their philosophy to extract economic benefit
efficiently from resources whose privatization is for some reason 
impractical, or to assign damages caused by substances of obvious human
origin, whose means of dispersal renders tracing their specific origin
effectively impossible?
    A class of examples that might motivate discussion is long-term
adverse medical effects of substances not previously suspected to be a
hazard, hence which no one bothered to trace while they were being
emitted.

                                -- Jay Freeman, First Extropian Squirrel

