From: Trygve Bauge <trygveb@lightning.powertech.no>
Date: Wed, 7 Dec 1994 10:24:09 +6000
Subject: [#94-12-264] Privatization of National Parks.

On Tue, 6 Dec 1994, Mark Fischer wrote:

> I *_AM_* searching for a plausible mechanism/explanation of how
> something _like_ today's National Parks/National Wildlife Refuges
> (among whose stated purposes is preservation for posterity,
> although it isn't always successfully implemented) could be done
> under this anarcho-capitalist-utopian thing. The Nature Conservancy,
> or something like it, works fine for smaller tracts of land.
> Big plots require Big money (especially if they are anywhere
> close to a population center), typically only available to national
> governments or international corporations. 

As far as I know, most forests in Norway and the earlier West Germany
are apparently privately owned.

If you own it, as a farmer or forester, there is an incentive to
maintain it.

In Norway, large areas are kept secluded as private hunting reserves.
The forest that covers most of our capital Oslo, and that extends as 
greenbelt into the latter, is also privately owned by a thimber company,
and an assortment of private farmers, though the public by right has 
full access to walk in this forest.

As far as the United States is concerned:
Large ski resorts could possibly branch out and own and provide
wilderness for an access fee.

Or national parks could privatize, incorporate and sell shares.

As long as clear cutting, tree planting and managed cultivation
go hand in hand there isn't much of a problem.
 
On the other hand if you are only operating a short term lease
on government land, then there is more of an incentive to grab and run.

And let us not forget that areas can be revegetated.
Wasn't the Washington and Jefferson memorial (the park in Washington DC)
city blocks, before they thore down the houses and built a park?
And in the Rocky Mountains there are wilderness areas, that once
were large and thriving mining towns with populations in the thousands.

Perpetual trusts are a menace to individual liberty,
and so are groupowned land that for all practical purposes
are tied up and not open for change.
 
It is important to not lock up land use for perpetuity,
but let the market forces work, so that areas can be developed
when it becomes highly valuable and thus highly profitable to do so.
And likewise so that areas can be bought up and redeveloped into
wilderness whenever the highest bidder turns out to want this.
Even if land has been set aside for wilderness, there should be
some mechanism whereby a present owner at any time, can reevaluate
the land use, in light of increasing demand for the land.
Selling off smaller plots in order to raise money to preserve 
or reclaim larger plots should also be an option.

In a free society areas can be bought up and turned into
wilderness whenever enough people value this to put up
enough of their own money to actually buy the land.

In a free society there will be lots of parks and wilderness,
but what land actually is used as a park or as a wilderness,
will change over time.

There is clearly a demand for parks and wilderness,
the task is to be sure that the demand is met by private 
rights respecting means, and that no land is tied down for
perpetuity.

