Subject: [#94-12-266] BASICS: Uploads

Thomas H. Smith wrote:
>
>Just one quick question...how much will the need for computing power be 
>reduced by the fact that an uploaded person won't need to control 
>his/her/it's breathing, digestion, cellular repair, etc?  Actually, I 
>guess I have two questions...also, how important is increased speed of 
>mentation?  I am not really that interested (I don't think, anyway) in 
>being able to think faster.  I'd rather be able to think MORE.

It depends on the uploading scenario. Most scenarios discussed center on 
the central nervous system (remember to upload the spinal cord! Its so 
irritating to forget how to walk), with some discussions about the role 
of the endocrine system too. Most models have only dealt with the CNS, 
and disregarded the rest. Presumably we have to start by giving the 
uploads virtual bodies, but these can probably be much simpler 
simulations (still complex, but hardly as complex as hi-fi simulation of 
a whole brain). There is several amusing passages in "Permutation City" 
by Greg Egan about the crudity of this simulation (vision is done by 
ray-tracing from the retina, eating produces the necessary sensory 
impressions and changes the simulated nutrient levels in the brain after 
a while etc). A guess will be that the upload brain will need the most 
power, followed by the body simulation which is more or less integral 
with the Virtual Reality it exists in. Creating a believable VR will 
require far less computing power than a brain.

The reason speed is so often discussed with uploading is that its much 
easier to achieve than increased mental ability. We know how to make a 
program run faster: buy a faster computer! Making a mind think more is 
much harder, and requires quite a bit of thought. But an uploaded mind is 
on the other hand much easier to experiment with (and with backups such 
experiments are much safer).


